all: debug detsched replays: positions, checkpoints, JSON output

For a program built with GOEXPERIMENT=detsched by the detsched
toolchain, whose runs are the same every time:

Every stop, breakpoint, tracepoint and return line ends with the run's
position as the runtime's stop lines write it, " at tD sN kN node X":
virtual time, switches, tick and, on a sim node, the node's name. The
expression evaluator has detsched.time, detsched.tick,
detsched.switches and detsched.node, which work on system stacks too,
for conditions. goroutines lists each goroutine's node and whether it
crashed or is paused, and -with/-without/-group take "node NAME".
api.Thread.Position and api.Goroutine.Node carry them to clients.

checkpoint records the run's position (tick, goroutine, PC and the
breakpoints' hit counts), and "restart cN" runs the program again to
it with the user's breakpoints disabled on the way: the runtime stops
the run at the checkpoint's tick through runtime.detDebug, a
GODETSTOP point that prints nothing, and the debugger continues to the
checkpoint's goroutine and PC. "restart tD", "restart sN" and
"restart kN" run to a point as GODETSTOP names one. rewind goes back
to the previous breakpoint stop of the session. These reuse the
commands rr recordings have.

dlv --json and "config output json" print one JSON object per line for
each command's output (print, stack, args, locals, goroutines, trace
and breakpoint hits, exits), so scripts need no filter over the text.

exit, and the end of an --init script, run a detsched program that is
still running to its end with its breakpoints cleared, so that it
prints its trace line, which says whether the run was the one
replayed; exit -k kills it instead. Exit messages carry the trace
line.

The wait reasons table for Go 1.27 has the two the detsched runtime
adds.

All of this is specific to detsched, except the JSON output mode, which
would suit upstream Delve without its detsched fields.

